  <static seed="428" source="Library/WHFreeman/Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition/14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables/14.4_Differentiability_and_Tangent_Planes/14.4.17.pg">
    <statement><p>Use the linear approximation of <m>f(x,y)=e^{2x^{2}+y}</m> at <m>(0,0)</m> to estimate</p>
<p><m>f(-0.02,0.01)\approx</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>1.01</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(a,b)=(0,0)</m>:

<me>\left. \begin{array}{lcl}
f(x,y)=e^{2x^{2}+y} \amp \amp  f(0,0)=1  \\
f_x(x,y)=4xe^{2x^{2}+y} \amp  \Rightarrow \amp  f_x(0,0)=0 \\
f_y(x,y)=e^{2x^{2}+y}  \amp \amp  f_y(0,0)=1
 \end{array} \right.</me>

The linear approximation is therefore
<me>f(h,k)\approx f(0,0)+f_x(0,0)h+f_y(0,0)k</me>
<me>f(h,k)\approx 1+k</me>
We substitute <m>h=-0.02, k=0.01</m> to obtain
<me>f(-0.02,0.01) \approx 1 + 1 \cdot 0.01  = 1.01</me>
The actual value is 
<me>f(-0.02,0.01)\approx 1.01086</me>





</solution>
